  "transcript": "# One-off Session on Defence Medical Services\n\n## How Ukraine has changed battlefield medicine\n> Medical facilities and staff are now at greater risk, evacuation is harder, and many casualties need prolonged care near where they were wounded.\n\nJesse Norman: Ukraine shows a different pattern of injuries and a different battlefield. Has the DMS learned the clinical lessons?\nDr Darren Mann: Yes—Ukrainian clinicians have shared experience directly. The big change is that medical facilities and staff can no longer be assumed safe.\nJesse Norman: Does that mean more protection for near-front medical units?\nDr Darren Mann: It means changing how we deliver care: more treatment needs to happen far forward, evacuation times are longer, and rail/road rather than air transport is routine.\nDr Katie Geary: We used to rely on rapid casevac. In Ukraine that’s constrained: surgical and trauma care must be pushed forward and become more ‘industrial’ in scale.\nDr Darren Mann: Also ethically, measures once seen as temporary—like tourniquets—may need to be permanent because limb salvage becomes impossible in prolonged care settings.\n\n\n## Capacity, private sector and voluntary organisations\n> The UK lacks surge capacity and joined-up planning; private and voluntary providers could help but need pre-arranged contracts and integration.\n\nEmma Lewell: Who should coordinate national planning so military, NHS, private and voluntary sectors work together?\nDr Darren Mann: The Cabinet Office, via civil contingencies, should lead a whole-of-nation plan; many transport and logistics assets are outside government visibility now.\nEd Button: International SOS can coordinate care, provide hands-on treatment and train staff. Other NATO partners already integrate industry as true partners; the UK lags behind.\nGraeme Downie: Can commercial and voluntary groups plug gaps at scale?\nEd Button: They can help, but their capacity isn’t infinite. The problem is converting capability awareness into pre-agreed contracts and funding—otherwise you’re at the back of the international queue.\nDr Katie Geary: Voluntary groups have a role, but they often lack clear link points into government planning. We must include them in exercises so we know what they can do.\nMichelle Scrogham: Would mobilisation of reservists affect private providers’ workforce?\nEd Button: Not significantly for reputable providers; commercial organisations plan to maintain capacity. But staffing at national scale would require regulatory adjustments.\n\n\n## Transport, stockpiles and regulation\n> We need pre-arranged access to trains, ships and aircraft, better stockpiles and flexible regulation for wartime practice—do it now, not after a crisis.\n\nMike Martin: Should the defence readiness Bill allow pre-arranged requisitioning of transport like trains and planes?\nDr Darren Mann: Yes—we should agree terms and service-level arrangements in peacetime rather than requisition at the last minute; requisition is legally strong but planning ahead gets better value.\nEd Button: Multimodal transport will be essential. The question is getting ahead of capacity and contracting now.\nMike Martin: What about stockpiles—how much should we hold?\nDr Darren Mann: Some sovereign stockpiling is needed—many critical items are manufactured overseas. A rule of thumb is around 120 days plus pre-agreed manufacturing and supply agreements.\nDerek Twigg: Has pandemic experience made procurement and regulation simpler for emergencies?\nDr Darren Mann: Not enough. Derogations—temporary relaxation of regulations—helped in covid and are needed again. But they must be planned, so clinicians can act without breaching professional rules in crisis.\nGraeme Downie: Can local charities be relied on?\nEd Button: They can help, but only if someone asks them and coordinates them ahead of time; otherwise their capacity is invisible until it’s needed.\n\n\n## Research, training and clinical priorities\n> Immediate R&D and training should focus on prolonged far-forward care, TCCC training, blood forward, non-compressible haemorrhage and tackling antimicrobial resistance.\n\nIan Roome: Which research priorities would save the most lives quickly?\nDr Darren Mann: Work that accepts prolonged in-field care: what can be done far forward, what equipment to send to the casualty, and honest prioritisation when resources are scarce.\nEd Button: TCCC training is a high-impact, low-cost priority—training large numbers reduces deaths on the frontline, as other nations have shown.\nDr Katie Geary: Focus on survivability and return-to-function metrics, point-of-care diagnostics for resistant infections, blood far forward and reach-back telemedicine to support far-forward personnel.\nIan Roome: What stops research reaching the frontlines?\nDr Katie Geary: Fragmented accountability: procurement, NICE, royal colleges and regulators all matter. You need a sponsored pathway from research to adoption and planned derogations for wartime use.\nIan Roome: Is pay and retention a problem for military medics?\nEd Button: Pay is a risk for retention, but broader support—housing, veteran treatment and career pathways—matters too. Many stay for purpose rather than pay.\n\n\n## Ethics, prioritisation and the need for a national debate\n> At scale, tough prioritisation choices are inevitable; government must lead an open discussion about wartime healthcare priorities and the moral choices involved.\n\nDerek Twigg: If thousands are wounded, will the NHS prioritise service personnel over civilians?\nDr Darren Mann: We need an honest national debate. In an existential war the priority is to return service personnel to the fight; that likely means two channels of care and use of private sector beds for military patients.\nDr Katie Geary: That’s a major cultural and ethical shift. Ukraine shows we may need to accept different outcomes and to plan for palliative care on the battlefield for some injuries.\nEd Button: All of this only works if exercised now. Without pre-planning you’re knitting things together at the last minute and will lose out on scarce resources.\nChair: If you could make the Government act, what would you ask and when?\nDr Darren Mann: Create a single organ with responsibility to integrate DMS, NHS, private and voluntary sectors and drive a national wartime medical plan—start now.\nEd Button: Provide fiscal support to DMS to enable pre-contracted private capacity and treat industry as a partner—not an enemy of public provision—immediately.\nDr Katie Geary: Commission an honest gap analysis against a Ukraine-type scenario, identify costs and partners, and don’t put the whole bill on DMS.\n",
